// CI-parity typecheck config.
//
// WHY THIS FILE EXISTS.
//
// `tsconfig.json` includes `.expo/types/**/*.ts`, which is where expo-router
// writes its GENERATED typed-routes declaration (`router.d.ts`). That
// directory is gitignored (`frontend/.gitignore:7`), and it is produced by the
// Expo dev server — `npx expo export` does NOT emit it (verified).
//
// The consequence, which cost this project a silently red CI for an entire
// phase: `npx tsc --noEmit` on a developer machine checks a DIFFERENT PROGRAM
// than the same command in CI. Locally `useSegments()` resolves to a union of
// real route tuples; in CI's fresh clone the declaration is absent and it
// degrades to `[string]`, so an ordinary `segments[1]` read becomes
// `TS2493: Tuple type '[string]' of length '1' has no element at index '1'`.
// Local said 0 errors, CI said 2, and three phase gates were closed on the
// local number.
//
// This config removes `.expo/types` from the include list, so
// `npm run typecheck:ci` reproduces CI's exact view on any machine, with or
// without a dev server having ever run. CI uses it too — not because CI needs
// the file (the declaration is absent there regardless) but so that what CI
// checks is stated explicitly instead of depending on the accidental absence
// of a gitignored artefact.
//
// The rule this implies for source code: application code must typecheck
// WITHOUT typed routes. Typed routes stay a local developer nicety, never a
// load-bearing input to the gate.
//
// Maintenance note: `include` and `exclude` are declared explicitly so gitignored
// `expo-env.d.ts`, `dist/`, `playwright-report/`, and `test-results/` never alter CI parity;
// tracked `types/expo.d.ts` supplies `expo/types` identically in local and CI builds.
{
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  "include": ["**/*.ts", "**/*.tsx"],
  "exclude": ["node_modules", "**/node_modules", "dist", "build", "expo-env.d.ts", "playwright-report", "test-results"]
}
